Hunt_Chain — Complete Project Picture
1. What Hunt_Chain actually is
Hunt_Chain is not supposed to be one giant vulnerability scanner.
The architecture is a chain of specialized security-testing components:
Bug Bounty / Authorized Assessment
             │
             ▼
      PROJECT 1
      ScopeGuard
             │
       Authorization
             │
             ▼
      PROJECT 2
   Recon & Attack Surface
             │
      Attack Surface
             │
             ▼
      PROJECT 3
 Vulnerability Testing
             │
       Potential Findings
             │
             ▼
      PROJECT 4
 Validation + Controlled
 Impact/Exploitation
             │
      Confirmed Findings
             │
             ▼
      PROJECT 5
 Findings & Reporting
             │
             ▼
       Final Report

The repository README describes the same fundamental philosophy: each project has a specific responsibility and produces structured information consumed by the next stage. GitHub
2. PROJECT 1 — ScopeGuard
Purpose
Determine whether a target is authorized before anything else happens.
ScopeGuard takes a manually prepared, machine-readable bug-bounty scope and classifies a target as:
IN_SCOPE
OUT_OF_SCOPE
UNKNOWN
CONFLICT

The important security principle is fail closed:
Cannot determine authorization
        ↓
DO NOT TEST

The GitHub README describes ScopeGuard as a deterministic target authorization engine. GitHub
What Project 1 provides
Scope definition
      ↓
Parsing
      ↓
Normalization
      ↓
Target matching
      ↓
Authorization decision
      ↓
authorization_result.json

V1 boundary
Your established V1 rule remains:
No Android / iOS / mobile application testing in V1.
That is explicitly reflected in the Project 3 family specification as well. GitHub
3. PROJECT 2 — Recon & Attack Surface Suite
This is your discovery and mapping layer.
Project 2 receives an authorized target and builds an Attack Surface Model V1.
The repository currently documents these V1 capabilities: GitHub
Discovery
- Certificate Transparency discovery
- Asset discovery
- Asset normalization
- Deduplication
- DNS resolution
- DNS analysis
- Wildcard DNS detection
- Potential dangling CNAME indicators
Infrastructure intelligence
- Response SHA-256 hashing
- Technology fingerprinting
- Infrastructure intelligence
- Shared-IP awareness
- Correlation
Output
attack_surface.json
recon_report.txt

Important Project 2 restriction
Project 2 does not perform vulnerability exploitation.
The repository explicitly excludes:
- SQL injection testing
- XSS exploitation
- Authentication attacks
- Password attacks
- Nuclei scanning
- WAF bypass
- Aggressive brute force
- DNS takeover exploitation
- Full cloud enumeration
Those belong later in the chain. GitHub
So:
P1 = Can I test it?
P2 = What exists?
P3 = What might be vulnerable?

